<<~ ahu slot=`#/land-queue-design-sketch`>>

! land-queue — design sketch (Queue-Wright, 2026-11-01)

Worktree `~/.lares-scratch/land-queue/wt` · branch `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--land-queue` · cut at 13d24a218.
Marks: [MEASURED] ran today · [READ] code or doc read today · [INFER] reasoning, a red must prove it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-shape-in-one-breath`>>

!! The shape in one breath

`tools/land-train.mjs` gains a QUEUE mode and keeps two others. A queued lane enters on its own CI green. The tool
derives every base, builds the train as the CHAINED LAND COMMITS themselves (house message, ` — merge PR #` subject,
parents prev-land + lane head) with `git merge-tree --write-tree` (no worktree, no install, no build), pushes the
chain head to `<span>--train-<n>`, reads that sha's CI with ci-verdict, and on green fast-forwards the span to that
exact sha under a lease on the head it read. No new commit ever lands that CI did not read.

* DIRECT stays as it stands (the control): one lane whose head holds the span head lands on the lane CI's own read.
* QUEUE (new default for every other land): cloud verify on the merge result.
* `--local` keeps today's battery as the fallback verifier, run over the same chain head.

[MEASURED] `git merge-tree --write-tree <span-prev> <lane-head>` reproduced the tree of the last 8 span lands
exactly, the two train-built ones (3726248e5, ece08dcd1) among them. git 2.43 on the box.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/why-the-chain-makes-bisection-free`>>

!! Why the chain makes bisection free

Commit i of the chain carries the tree of span + lanes 1..i. Every prefix train therefore already stands as an
intermediate commit of the full train, with its own sha. Bisecting a red train pushes an EXISTING chain commit to a
probe branch and reads its CI; no rebuild. A green prefix lands at once by fast-forward (it descends from the span).
The first red prefix j names lane j the culprit (prefix j-1 read green); lane j drops, lanes j+1..k re-chain on the
new span head and verify again. Halving over k lanes costs ⌈log2 k⌉ probes; a single-lane train has no bisect.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/threat-sketch-every-authority-input`>>

!! Threat sketch — every authority input

| Input | Source | What proves it | Law lives in | Fails closed as |h
| Lane list, owner order, PR, message file | controller argv / enqueue | head = `origin/<span>--<suffix>` exactly; message non-empty AND its subject carries ` — merge PR #<pr>` (today unchecked: a land without the marker reads later as a road commit) | `parseSpec` + preflight | exit 3 head moved · exit 2 message |
| Lane green | GitHub CI on the lane branch | ci-verdict at the exact lane head (ignored-only head carried by a green lane ancestor, as today) | `classifyLane` | exit 8 not green · 10 UNREADABLE |
| Base | DERIVED: `merge-base origin/<span> <lane>` | no input; never typed | the chain builder | — (exit 9 burns) |
| Span head | `git fetch` → `origin/<span>` | CI green AT THAT SHA on the span OR on a `<span>--train-*` branch (`gh run list --commit <sha>` filtered by headBranch, so a deleted train branch still answers); an ignored-only head carried by a green ancestor (today's walk) | span gate (F2) | refuses to build: a red span would blame every lane in a bisect |
| Merge result | local `git merge-tree` | exit 0 with a tree; exit 1 names conflicted paths | chain builder | the lane drops, named (F5) |
| Train verdict | CI run on `<span>--train-<n>` | the run whose `headSha` IS the chain head; a run of any other sha never counts; the branch is pushed under a lease expecting it absent | ci-verdict `--json` | red → rerun-once then bisect (F4) · cancelled-clean → VOID exit 13, never a wait |
| Fast-forward | `git push --force-with-lease=<span>:<SP>` of the train sha | chain walks first-parent from the train head to SP exactly through k land commits, each `^2` = its lane head, each tree = merge-tree's | pre-push structure check | span moved → re-chain on the new head (F8); structure break → exit 5 |
| Security class | `tools/security-class-paths.json` read from the SPAN HEAD's tree (`git show <SP>:…`), never the lane's | a lane whose `diff --no-renames --name-only <base>..<head>` meets a glob is security-class; the table and land-train itself sit in the table | `securityClass()` | needs SHIP |
| SHIP | `~/.lares-scratch/controller/ship/<suffix>-<full-sha>.md`, controller-written | file exists for the FULL lane head sha, and its body names that sha and a `read:` line | `shipRefusal()` | exit 12, by lane name, before anything pushes (F6) |
| Queue state | `~/.lares-scratch/train/` ledger (train counter, sha → train branch, drops) | advisory only: every green re-reads GitHub; the ledger never stands for a verdict | — | a lost ledger costs a re-read, never a false land |
| Author | `git config user.name/email` | non-empty | as today | exit 2 |

Every wait ends on a result or a process exit. A train wait polls ci-verdict and ends on GREEN, RED, VOID (run
cancelled clean), a hard read error (exit 3), or UNREADABLE settled (six reads, exit 10). The one open-ended span
waits for a run to APPEAR after the push; it ends on the run or on the land process's exit, and the tool refuses
up front a train whose diff over SP changes ignored paths alone (no run would come; canon goes direct, item 8).
The sleeps between reads pace polling and decide nothing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/speculation-and-budget`>>

!! Speculation and budget

At most TWO train runs in flight (ci-ten's ten-job run, twenty-job account). Train size caps at 4 (flow-scout's
batch math: past 5 a red costs more than it saves). While train N verifies, lanes queued behind it chain on N's head
as train N+1 and push. N green → land N, then N+1 already descends from the span and lands on its own green. N red →
N+1's run gets cancelled (its base no longer lands) and re-chains after N's bisect settles. Bisect probes take a
slot ahead of a speculative train.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/exits`>>

!! Exits (alpha, no back-compat: exit 9 and the `<base>` positional burn)

0 every lane landed · 2 usage/identity/message/checkout · 3 a head moved · 5 nothing landed (every lane red) or the
chain broke · 6 disk/worktree cap (`--local` only) or a run in flight on the span · 8 a lane not green · 10 UNREADABLE
· 11 some landed, the rest dropped, each named with its failing jobs · 12 a security-class lane lacks its SHIP · 13 VOID.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/reds`>>

!! Reds (after the ruling; each its own commit, a control beside it)

# A lane lacking the span head lands through a train (red today: exit 9).
# A train's verdict reads the pushed train head's CI (a verdict stub keyed by branch+sha), never the local battery; CONTROL: a green on another sha of the train branch does not land.
# A red train of 3 drops the culprit (probe stub: prefix 1 green, prefix 2 red) and lands lanes 1 and 3; prefix 1 lands by fast-forward to its EXISTING chain sha.
# A security-class lane with no SHIP refuses by name (exit 12) and nothing pushes; CONTROL: the SHIP for the exact sha passes, one for another sha refuses.
# Train N+1 re-chains when N reds.
# CONTROL: DIRECT still lands a lane holding the span head, unchanged.
Plus: a message without ` — merge PR #<pr>` refuses (2); a cancelled-clean train run exits 13 and never waits.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/forks-two-rounds-with-a-sonnet-reader-settled-ru`>>

!! Forks — two rounds with a Sonnet reader (YANG), settled rules

Round one: the reader found eight holes. The worst three:
* H1: a red span reads green through the ignored-only carry, and that red then gets blamed on a lane.
* H2: held-out runs as decoration under cancel-in-progress.
* H3: cancelling the span CI would hang every `--wait` vigil.

Round two traded the revised leans below.

* ''F1 SETTLED, my lean flipped.'' The span's CI on the landed sha runs uncancelled, so the vigil and every reader stay as they are; the next land's push cancels it anyway. Only inside land-train, the span-head verdict reads the span's run at the sha, else the train branch's run at that sha (ledger first, else `gh run list --commit <sha> --workflow CI` filtered to train headBranches), checked by headSha. The ci-verdict CLI stays unchanged. [INFER: `--commit` with `--workflow` and no `--branch`; measure before the red.]
* ''F2 SETTLED.'' No CI pre-gate on the span head. The queue pauses when the newest COMPLETED whole held-out run on the span reads red (naming the sha it covered), or when more than M=3 lands stand past the last held-out-covered sha. That count also lets a held-out run finish despite cancel-in-progress.
* ''H1 rule (with F9).'' A culprit is named only at a green-then-red prefix pair whose green is a REAL run at that exact sha, never a carry. When the span head has no real run, prefix 0 (the span head) is probed on a red, with the same rerun-once, before any lane gets blamed. A red prefix 0 pauses the queue as "SPAN RED" and drops no lane.
* ''F3 SETTLED.'' Enqueue entries come from the controller, and `run` re-validates every field as argv does (head = origin ref, message marker, SHIP). The lock is a pid file; a takeover RESUMES by re-reading the remote `--train-*` refs and their verdicts, never cancelling blind and never trusting the ledger. Train branches carry the sha: `<span>--train-<n>-<sha9>`.
* ''F4 SETTLED.'' On any red, `gh run rerun --failed` runs once. The wait ends when the run's `attempt` advances or the run completes; then it reads again.
* ''F5 SETTLED.'' A conflict drops lane j by name, together with every later lane whose head contains lane j's commits.
* ''F6 SETTLED.'' A security-class lane with no SHIP for its exact full sha refuses at enqueue and again at chain build: exit 12, nothing pushes. Every mode checks, DIRECT and `--local` included. Said plainly: SHIP guards against mistakes, and a hostile same-user process could forge one.
* ''F7 SURVIVES → controller'' (see the hand-back). Both options take the round-two path seed: itself, land-train.mjs, ci-verdict.mjs, ci-ignored-paths.*, held-out-cells.mjs, witness-all.sh, evergreen-boundary.mjs, battery.sh, provision-worktree.mjs, .github/workflows/**, package.json, pnpm-lock.yaml, pnpm-workspace.yaml, .npmrc, .gitattributes (merge drivers move merge-tree), packages/lararium-keyhive/**. The diff runs `--no-renames --raw`, so a mode change or a symlink retargeted onto a table path counts too.
* ''F8 SETTLED.'' Canon pushes hold while a train flies. `land-train status` prints the in-flight trains and exits nonzero while one flies, so a canon push script can gate on it. If the span moves anyway, the train re-chains and re-verifies, and never lands an unread tree.
* ''F9 SETTLED.'' The base walk and exit 9 retire in QUEUE mode, and only together with the H1 rule. `--local` keeps the walk.
* ''H5 rule.'' The run-appearance wait stays open and decides nothing. Each round it re-lists by `--commit`, confirms the origin ref equals the chain head, and prints `NO RUN YET <sha>`, so the controller can see it and stop the process. An ignored-only train refuses up front.
* ''H8 rule.'' `--depth` (default 2) counts every train and probe run in flight, plus the span's own active runs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/measures-before-the-reds-ship-stays-a-mistake-gu`>>

!! Measures before the reds (controller ruling 2026-11-01: F7 (a); SHIP stays a mistake guard)

(1) [MEASURED] `gh run list --commit <sha> --workflow CI --json headBranch,attempt,…` with no `--branch` returns runs
from any branch (0dd843a27 → run 41280151942 on `--recut-rolls`, headBranch, attempt 1). The span-head reading by sha
across the span and its train branches holds as designed. `attempt` is a list field, so F4's wait reads it directly.

(2) [MEASURED, 7 lands] Pushing an existing sha to a second ref raises a run there when the push's own diff touches a
non-ignored path, and raises none when it changes nothing:
* 3726248e5 (train-built) and ece08dcd1 (train-built) are span shas pushed afterwards to their lane branches, whose
tree differed. Each raised a lane-branch CI run, which land-train cancelled.
* 13d24a218, ec3c990be, 5535ebe8c and b333ed731 are DIRECT lands, whose tree equals the lane head's, so the lane push
changed no file. None raised any run: paths-ignore matches an empty diff.
* a39b1f5df, from before paths-ignore landed, raised runs anyway.

So an already-seen sha raises a run on a new ref. The fast-forward of the span (before = SP, after = the train head,
whose diff holds the lanes' code) raises a span CI run and a held-out run at the train sha. F1 (span CI uncancelled)
and F2 (held-out per land on the span) stand unchanged. [INFER, unmeasured: the span push itself; the lane pushes
above stand as the analogue.]

The held-out pause, measured against this: right after a land, the newest COMPLETED held-out run covers an older sha,
and the new run sits in flight at the train sha. The count rule reads lands past the covered sha, so a run of lands
under held-out's ~40 min (each cancelling the last) pauses the queue at M=3. The pause then waits on the in-flight run
(a result) and never on a clock.

Two consequences for the build:
* A lane whose diff changes ignored paths alone would raise no train or probe run (a dead `NO RUN YET`). The queue
refuses such a lane at enqueue: a meme-only lane that holds the span head lands DIRECT, and canon goes direct.
* The prefix-0 probe needs a real run at SP. It reads any completed CI run at that sha (span or train branch) and
waits on one in flight. Only when no run exists does it dispatch `gh workflow run CI --ref <span>`, which ci.yml
documents for exactly this case.

Side finding: after a DIRECT land, the cancel loop waits three rounds for lane-branch runs that never come (equal
tree, paths-ignore). It costs time and decides nothing; the cure runs that loop only after a train land.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/as-built-three-points-the-design-left-open`>>

!! As built (the cure), three points the design left open

* ''The queue's span push skips the "no run in flight on the span" gate.'' DIRECT and `--local` keep it. A queue land
fast-forwards the span to a sha whose own CI the train run already read. Held-out cancel-in-progress is bounded by the
M=3 gate, so waiting out the span's runs (~40 min) would re-impose the serial land the queue exists to remove.
This changes canon #/the-lanes ("the span pushes only while no run flies on it"); the canon text goes to the controller.
* ''Depth.'' The first train on the span always builds. A speculative one builds only while
trains + the span's active runs &lt; `--depth`. Counting the span's runs against even the first train would make each
run after a land wait ~40 min for a train that GitHub's runner queue would simply hold.
* ''The DIRECT land no longer waits on lane-branch runs to cancel'' (measure (2): that push raises none).

<<~/ahu>>

